a. Write a program for routing to different pages using react router
  React Router is a special library in React that helps you move between pages in your web 
  app without refreshing the whole page.
  Normally, websites reload the page each time you click a link — but React Router lets you 
  switch pages instantly because it uses the Single Page Application (SPA) concept.
  It uses a few main components:  BrowserRouter → wraps the entire app and enables routing.
   Routes → holds all your page routes.
   Route → defines which component should show for each URL.
   Link → used to create clickable navigation links (instead of <a> tags).

PROGRAM
  import React from "react";
  import { BrowserRouter as Router, Routes, Route, Link } from "react-router-dom";
  function Home() {
  return <h2>This is Home Page</h2>;
  }
  function About() {
  return <h2>This is About Page</h2>;
  }
  function Contact() {
  return <h2>This is Contact Page</h2>;
  }
  export default function App() { 
  return (
  <Router>
  <div>
  <h1>React Router Example</h1>
  <nav>
  <Link to="/">Home</Link> |{" "}
  <Link to="/about">About</Link> |{" "}
  <Link to="/contact">Contact</Link>
  </nav>
  <hr />
  <Routes>
  <Route path="/" element={<Home />} />
  <Route path="/about" element={<About />} />
  <Route path="/contact" element={<Contact />} />
  </Routes>
  </div>
  </Router>
  );
  } .


Output: b. Write a program for updating the screen.
  1. React automatically updates the screen when state changes.
  2. Components can manage their own state using useState().
  3. Changing state causes React to re-render the affected part.
  4. React uses the Virtual DOM to detect and update only changed elements.
  5. This makes React apps fast, interactive, and dynamic.
  For Updating Screen Example
  Create a new file inside src/ folder with the name of UpdateScreen.js


PROGRAM
  import React, { useState } from "react";
  function UpdateScreen() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  return (
  <div style={{ textAlign: "center" }}>
  <h1>React Screen Update Example</h1>
  <h2>Count: {count}</h2>
  <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Increase</button>
  <button onClick={() => setCount(count - 1)}>Decrease</button>
  </div>
  );
  }
  export default UpdateScreen;
  Now open App.js:
  import UpdateScreen from "./UpdateScreen";
  function App() {
   return (
   <div>
   <UpdateScreen />
   </div>
   );
  }
  export default App;
